          "text": "Sure \u2014 here\u2019s a reliable recipe for a classic meat lasagne (Bolognese + b\u00e9chamel) that serves about 6\u20138.\n\nIngredients\n- For the ragu (meat sauce):\n  - 2 tbsp olive oil\n  - 1 onion, finely chopped\n  - 1 carrot, finely chopped\n  - 1 celery stalk, finely chopped\n  - 2 garlic cloves, minced\n  - 500 g (1.1 lb) ground beef (or mix beef + pork)\n  - 120 ml (\u00bd cup) dry red or white wine (optional)\n  - 800 g (28 oz) canned crushed tomatoes or passata\n  - 2 tbsp tomato paste (optional, for richness)\n  - 1 tsp dried oregano or Italian seasoning\n  - Salt and pepper to taste\n  - A pinch of sugar (if tomatoes taste acidic)\n- For the b\u00e9chamel:\n  - 60 g (4 tbsp) butter\n  - 60 g (\u00bd cup) plain/all\u2011purpose flour\n  - 900 ml (3 \u00be cups) milk, warmed\n  - Salt, white pepper or black pepper, and a pinch of nutmeg\n- Assembly and topping:\n  - 9\u201312 lasagne sheets (fresh or dried/no\u2011boil as indicated on package)\n  - 150\u2013200 g (1\u20131\u00bd cups) grated Parmesan or Pecorino\n  - 200\u2013250 g (7\u20139 oz) shredded mozzarella (optional, for a melty top)\n  - Butter or oil for the baking dish\n\nEstimated times\n- Active prep: 30\u201345 minutes\n- Simmering ragu: 30\u201360 minutes (longer = better flavor)\n- Bake: 35\u201345 minutes\n- Total: ~1.5\u20132.5 hours depending on simmer time\n\nMethod\n1. Make the ragu:\n   - Heat olive oil in a large pan over medium heat. Add onion, carrot, celery and cook until softened (6\u20138 minutes). Add garlic for 1 minute.\n   - Add ground meat, break up with a spoon, and brown until no longer pink.\n   - Pour in wine (if using) and let it reduce for a few minutes.\n   - Stir in crushed tomatoes, tomato paste, oregano, salt, pepper and a pinch of sugar. Lower heat and simmer gently for at least 30 minutes (45\u201360 minutes improves depth). Stir occasionally; add a splash of water if it becomes too thick.\n\n2. Make the b\u00e9chamel:\n   - Melt butter in a saucepan over medium heat. Whisk in flour and cook 1\u20132 minutes to make a roux (don\u2019t let it brown).\n   - Gradually whisk in warm milk until smooth. Bring to a gentle simmer, whisking, until thickened to a sauce that coats the back of a spoon (about 5\u20138 minutes).\n   - Season with salt, pepper and a pinch of nutmeg. Remove from heat.\n\n3. Preheat oven to 180\u00b0C (350\u00b0F).\n\n4. Assemble:\n   - Lightly grease a 23 x 33 cm (9 x 13 in) baking dish.\n   - Spread a thin layer of ragu on the bottom, then a thin layer of b\u00e9chamel.\n   - Place lasagne sheets to cover (trim if needed). Add a layer of ragu, a drizzle or spread of b\u00e9chamel, and a sprinkle of grated Parmesan and a little mozzarella.\n   - Repeat with 2\u20133 more layers finishing with b\u00e9chamel and a generous layer of Parmesan and mozzarella on top.\n\n5. Bake:\n   - Cover with foil (lightly greased or tented so cheese doesn\u2019t stick) and bake for 25\u201330 minutes.\n   - Remove foil and bake another 10\u201315 minutes until bubbly and golden.\n   - Let rest 10\u201315 minutes before slicing so layers set.\n\nVariations and tips\n- No\u2011boil sheets: follow package instructions; you may need slightly more sauce and no pre-boiling.\n- Vegetarian option: replace meat with a mix of saut\u00e9ed mushrooms, spinach, roasted vegetables or a lentil tomato mix.\n- For richer flavor, use a mix of milk and a little cream in the b\u00e9chamel or add a parmesan rind to the ragu while simmering.\n- Make\u2011ahead/freezing: Assemble but don\u2019t bake, cover tightly and freeze. Bake from frozen (add ~20\u201330 minutes) or thaw overnight and bake as directed. Baked lasagne also reheats well covered at 160\u2013170\u00b0C (325\u2013340\u00b0F) until heated through.\n- Resting is important: it makes slicing neater.\n\nServing\n- Serve with a simple green salad and crusty bread. A light red wine or sparkling water pairs well.\n\nIf you want, I can scale this recipe for a different number of servings, convert to metric/imperial only, or give a vegetarian or gluten\u2011free variation."
